{"model":"Open scalar sites 0..n-1 and two occupations i<j. Vacuum exterior flux, with energized links [i,j). In a cycle apply every even bond, then every odd bond. Real-gauge single-occupation rotation [[c,-s],[s,c]], (c,s)=(3,4)/5 or (4,3)/5. Vacuum and double occupation unchanged. Multiply by w on every energized link once, after both layers, with w=exp(-i tau sigma a/(2 epsilon)); selected phases fix this product. A retimed control applies the phase after each layer.","mixtures":"c is a fixed preparation fraction of the permanently path-recorded sector. Q is the coherent squared read in the unmarked sector; T is the recorded branch's probability kernel. Finite t gives R=(1-c)Q+cP with P=(T^t)_{ii}. Conditional sensitivity entries are hypothetical thresholds, not measured returns. The physical return is to both original sites and hence to their unique vacuum-sector flux pattern.","pointer":"One fresh bit per active bond per cycle records hop/stay. For a fixed localized input the complete bit string specifies the unique trajectory. A finite binary register of (n-1)t bits suffices at each finite horizon. Tracing permanent orthogonal records adds their squares; retained signed paths with a common final Gauss string interfere without the pointer. Long-time Markov statistics may also be generated by dephasing or discarding freshly reset pointers. A single fixed finite reversible record cannot store arbitrarily long distinct histories.","proof":"The site exclusion graph is connected: repeatedly move the left particle left to 0, then the right to 1. Each such hop and its reverse have positive one-cycle probability by holding at every other gate. Every configuration has a positive self-loop, and every local squared gate and their product are doubly stochastic. Thus the primitive matrix powers tend to the uniform matrix on binom(n,2) states. Finite-dimensional unitary recurrence supplies forward t_j tending to infinity with W^t_j approaching identity. Along those horizons the mixture return tends to 1-c+c/binom(n,2). For n=2 both laws give one. The observation law and apparatus stability across horizons are hypotheses.","geometry":[{"sites":2,"two_particle_dimension":1,"stationary_return":"1","one_cycle_min_hold":1.0,"number_directed_elementary_hops":0,"smallest_elementary_hop":null,"smallest_strictly_positive_power":1,"eigenvalue_moduli_descending":[1.0],"max_deviation_from_uniform_at_64":0.0,"unitary_unit_modulus_error":0.0},{"sites":3,"two_particle_dimension":3,"stationary_return":"1/3","one_cycle_min_hold":0.23040000000000005,"number_directed_elementary_hops":4,"smallest_elementary_hop":0.1296,"smallest_strictly_positive_power":2,"eigenvalue_moduli_descending":[1.0000000000000002,0.4179722576459079,0.18757225764590818],"max_deviation_from_uniform_at_64":3.6637359812630166e-15,"unitary_unit_modulus_error":6.661338147750939e-16},{"sites":4,"two_particle_dimension":6,"stationary_return":"1/6","one_cycle_min_hold":0.08294400000000002,"number_directed_elementary_hops":12,"smallest_elementary_hop":0.046655999999999996,"smallest_strictly_positive_power":2,"eigenvalue_moduli_descending":[0.9999999999999998,0.5930074461452761,0.47840189404320027,0.28000000000000014,0.13220744614527713,0.04588610595679982],"max_deviation_from_uniform_at_64":4.3021142204224816e-15,"unitary_unit_modulus_error":7.771561172376096e-16},{"sites":5,"two_particle_dimension":10,"stationary_return":"1/10","one_cycle_min_hold":0.05308416000000002,"number_directed_elementary_hops":24,"smallest_elementary_hop":0.029859840000000002,"smallest_strictly_positive_power":3,"eigenvalue_moduli_descending":[0.9999999999999992,0.7131325217406139,0.5252656493743652,0.32743893000332847,0.25009805617986985,0.23943396101130396,0.1526212294226578,0.1099374907326364,0.06123209684155203,0.03077378070997442],"max_deviation_from_uniform_at_64":1.5262424657436213e-10,"unitary_unit_modulus_error":2.6645352591003757e-15},{"sites":6,"two_particle_dimension":15,"stationary_return":"1/15","on